T9 — Curated synthetic Clostridium promoters (literature)
=========================================================

DELIVERABLE the pipeline consumes:  literature_promoters.tsv
  (one merged, model-ready file; staged to inputs/literature_promoters.tsv.
   Held OUT of training -> used for cross-species external eval, T24/T26.)

Build:  python build_literature_promoters.py   (re-run to regenerate from the raw TSVs)


Sources (raw curation kept in this folder for provenance — do not delete)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
  literature_promoters - C. acetobutylicum - 1.tsv  Yang 2017    (35)  ACS Synth Biol, PMID 28602076
  literature_promoters - C. acetobutylicum - 2.tsv  Mordaka 2018 (33)  ACS Synth Biol, PMID 29320851
  literature_promoters - C. phytofermentans.tsv     Rostain 2022 (25)  ACS Synth Biol, PMID 36427328
  -> 93 promoters total.


What build_literature_promoters.py does (and why)
-------------------------------------------------
1. MERGE the three per-study TSVs into one file. (The pipeline expects a single
   literature_promoters.tsv; promoter strengths are in different units per study,
   which is why curation was originally split — see strength_unit below.)

2. TRIM the cloning scaffold from the C. acetobutylicum constructs. Every Yang/
   Mordaka sequence is ~171 bp but ends in a CONSTANT 120 bp reporter scaffold
   (5'UTR + RBS AGGAGG + SalI GTCGAC + XbaI TCTAGA + BamHI GGATCC) that is identical
   across all 68 constructs. Left in, ~70% of every "promoter" is the vector and a
   DNA model would learn the scaffold, not the promoter. Trimming leaves the ~50 bp
   variable promoter (the -35..-10 region; the TATAAT -10 box is retained). The
   scaffold is asserted at the 3' end of every aceto sequence, so a future curation
   edit can't silently mistrim. C. phytofermentans seqs have no scaffold (already ~51 bp).

3. STRIP alignment gaps. 7 aceto sequences contain '-' characters — gaps the source
   alignment inserted around the -35/-10 to keep the -10 box in register. A variant's
   true sequence is the ungapped string, so they are removed to give clean ACGT.

Nothing curated is altered or dropped: strengths are kept verbatim in their original
units, and the untouched input string is preserved in `original_sequence`.


Output schema (literature_promoters.tsv, tab-separated)
-------------------------------------------------------
  promoter_id          <source>_<original name>, unique
  original_name        name as curated (e.g. 100-1, pPM36-79%-18*, Pcphy1)
  species              Clostridium acetobutylicum | Clostridium phytofermentans
  in_genus             True (acetobutylicum) | False (phytofermentans, see note)
  sequence             cleaned, model-ready promoter (ACGT only, ~49-52 bp)
  seq_len              length of `sequence`
  strength             curated value, verbatim; blank where the study reported N/A
  strength_unit        relative_to_Pthl (Yang/Mordaka) | mean_AU (Rostain)
  n_gaps_removed       alignment-gap '-' chars removed (0 for all but 7 rows)
  scaffold_trimmed_bp  120 for aceto, 0 for phytofermentans
  source               Yang2017 | Mordaka2018 | Rostain2022
  label                1 (all positives)
  taxonomy_note        non-empty only for phytofermentans (see below)
  citation             full literature citation
  original_sequence    untouched curated input string (lossless provenance)


Taxonomy caveat
---------------
C. phytofermentans has been reclassified to *Lachnoclostridium phytofermentans*; it sits
OUTSIDE Clostridium sensu stricto (genus Clostridium, taxon 1485 — our target's genus).
It is flagged in_genus=False so eval (T24/T26) can stratify in- vs out-of-genus. The 25
phytofermentans promoters are still valid curated positives; just not in-genus.
C. acetobutylicum IS in genus Clostridium (in_genus=True).
